    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag121</id>
    <title>The Impact of Commercial Real Estate Regulations on U.S. Output</title>
    <updated>2026-11-11T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Fil Babalievsky, Kyle F Herkenhoff, Lee E Ohanian, Edward C Prescott</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Review of Economic Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Commercial real estate accounts for about 20% of the U.S. capital stock and is highly regulated. Yet relatively little is known about how these regulations affect economic activity or consumer welfare. This paper develops a spatial general equilibrium model of the U.S. economy with commercial real estate regulations and congestion effects, the latter providing a rationale for such regulations. The model constructs a panel of commercial real estate regulation indices for major U.S. metropolitan areas by exploiting variation in the share of parcel value attributable to land versus structures in tax assessor records compiled by Cotality. We find that a moderate relaxation of commercial real estate building regulations across U.S. cities generates large allocative efficiency gains, increasing output by 2.7% to 5.3% and welfare by 2.8% to 8.0% of lifetime consumption. We then extract the component of our regulatory indices that covaries with specific zoning attributes in LightBox data, including zoning height limits, and use it to simulate local deregulation policies. Relaxing height limits in New York City raises its output by 2.9%.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag121"/>
    <category term="Review of Economic Studies"/>
    <published>2026-11-11T00:00:00+00:00</published>
