    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjag057</id>
    <title>Labor Market Tightness and the Reversal of Inequality Trends in Germany</title>
    <updated>2026-11-11T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Christian Dustmann, Martin Friedrich, Carl Gergs, Uta Schönberg</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;The Quarterly Journal of Economics&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Germany experienced a sharp rise in wage inequality over the two decades leading up to the Great Recession. However, a remarkable trend reversal occurred in 2010—well before the introduction of the minimum wage in 2015—when wages at the bottom of the distribution began to grow more rapidly than those higher up. This paper attributes the reversal to an increasingly tight labor market after the Great Recession, supported by strong domestic demand, and operating through several interrelated and mutually reinforcing channels. Consistent with this hypothesis, job-to-job transition rates and between-job wage growth rose markedly after the recession, especially for workers at the lower rungs of the job ladder. Concurrently, workers increasingly moved from low-paying to higher-paying firms, compressing the worker-weighted distribution of firm wage premia. Tighter labor markets also reduced assortative matching between workers and firms, lowered the skill premium, and weakened firms’ monopsony power.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjag057"/>
    <category term="The Quarterly Journal of Economics"/>
    <published>2026-11-11T00:00:00+00:00</published>
