    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261510182</id>
    <title>Digital platforms meet artificial intelligence: Implications for POM research and practice</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Edward Anderson, Nitin Joglekar, Sunil Mithas, Geoffrey Parker, Yinliang (Ricky) Tan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Digital platforms are changing how firms design, coordinate, and govern operations by integrating artificial intelligence, blockchain, digital sensing and digital twins, data infrastructure, and additive manufacturing. This article develops a forward-looking research agenda for production and operations management by distinguishing enabling technologies from the platforms, workflows, and business models through which they are deployed. We organize the agenda around a sequence linking technological capability, operational manifestation, mechanism, outcomes, and boundary conditions. The relevant mechanisms include prediction, information visibility, verification, orchestration, monitoring, human override, and decision rights. We articulate how these mechanisms may create operational value through improvements in productivity, quality, responsiveness, resilience, compliance, and customer experience, enabling firms to capture financial value through revenue growth, cost reduction, risk avoidance, and improved asset utilization. We identify enduring operations management themes that emerging technologies may reshape, and develop five forward-looking paths involving artificial intelligence-enabled business-to-business platforms, physical–digital integration, agentic service workflows, data and artificial intelligence governance, and platform-enabled resilience under geopolitical disruption. Finally, we discuss theoretical and methodological approaches that can support cumulative research.</content>
