    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/00222429261510754</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: How Gig Sellers Do the Hustle</title>
    <updated>2026-11-16T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Eileen Fischer, Sandy D. Jap</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Marketing&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We conceptualize and develop theoretical insights related to a neglected category of market actors: gig sellers. Gig sellers are independent agents who sell products on behalf of a specific brand with marketing support. They face semi-entrepreneurial opportunities, meaning that while they are entirely responsible for managing the customer sales funnel, they do not develop new products or services like conventional entrepreneurs. They are compensated solely by sales commissions and do not receive salaries or benefits like organizational salesreps. Drawing on a large set of qualitative data from effective agents, we inductively and abductively draw inspiration both from literature and our analyses to unpack how these agents seize the opportunity to accomplish what is important to them. We find prominent reliance on effectual versus predictive practices is crucial to satisfactory achievement of outcomes. We expand our understanding of the range of outcomes that gig selling may result in, whether it be to remain in gig sales (enacting long-term loyalty, switching teams, or enriching) or enter alternative opportunity corridors (pursuing entrepreneurship, seeking employment). Our theoretical insights contrast with findings regarding salespeople in institutional settings and other categories of independent agents. Practical implications for agents and organizations are developed.</content>
