    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.70143</id>
    <title>Silent female representation and tech entrepreneurial project evaluation: The role of evaluators' genders</title>
    <updated>2026-11-17T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Yan “Anthea” Zhang, Lun Li, Jizhen Li, Xiwei Yi</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Management Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We examine how the nature of female representation in tech entrepreneurial projects may be related to the evaluations of the projects. We argue thatsilent female representation—i.e., women are present but do not speak in representing projects—may reinforce the stereotyped view of women, resulting in lower project evaluations relative to male‐only projects, whereas active female representation may not lead to evaluation penalty. We further predict that the negative association between silent female representation and project evaluation is stronger when the evaluators are women than when they are men. Conducted in a unique setting wherein female and male evaluators on panels, independently and confidentially, rated funding applications represented by various mixes of male and female entrepreneurs, our study provides evidence that is consistent with these predictions.Managerial SummaryIt is widely recognized that female entrepreneurs face greater challenges than their male counterparts when seeking external financial resources in the tech sector. This funding disparity may start from evaluation disparity. We find that projects with silent female representation—i.e., women are present but do not speak—receive lower evaluations than male‐only projects, whereas projects with active female representation receive evaluations comparable to male‐only projects. We also find that female evaluators are more likely than male evaluators to penalize projects with silent female representation. Our research offers valuable insights into strategies for fostering gender equality in traditionally male‐dominated fields.</content>
