    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjag059</id>
    <title>Earnings Instability</title>
    <updated>2026-11-17T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Peter Ganong, Pascal Noel, Christina Patterson, Joseph Vavra, Alexander Weinberg</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;The Quarterly Journal of Economics&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper uses high-frequency administrative data to show that most U.S. workers experience substantial month-to-month fluctuations in pay, even within ongoing employment relationships. Monthly earnings instability is pervasive but is masked in annual data. Moreover, this instability is unequally distributed: lower-income, hourly workers face more instability than higher-income, salaried workers. Firm-driven fluctuations in hours account for a large share of this instability. Monthly earnings instability, in turn, increases spending volatility and job separations. Short-term earnings risk is therefore a significant feature of the labor market and falls disproportionately on the most financially fragile workers.</content>
