    <id>https://doi.org/10.1111/joms.70194</id>
    <title>May I Have Your Attention, Please? Debating the Ontology of Attention in Organizational Research</title>
    <updated>2026-11-17T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Paolo Aversa, Christopher Wickert</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Management Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Few constructs in management research have proved as generative as organizational attention, and few have been left so largely unexamined at their core. ThisPoint‐Counterpointdebate brings a dormant question into the open:what exactly is attention?ThePointargues for an ontological reorientation of the Attention‐Based View from attention quantity to attention quality, reconceptualizing attention as a set of sociomaterial practices of attending that are crescive and differentiated rather than limited and homogeneous. OneCounterpointdefends the ontological integrity of attention as scarce, selective, and situated, warning that a crescive ontology dissolves the very constraint from which attention derives its explanatory power. The otherCounterpointcharts an expansive third way, tracing the contested assumptions to Herbert Simon's legacy and proposing to broaden the Attention‐Based View towards competition, thicker situations, and possible attentional abundance. In this introduction to the debate, we present the three positions and reflect on what their disagreement teaches us about ontological disputes in management theory more generally. We distil three tests – of phenomena, of explanation, and of genealogy – that scholars can apply when deciding whether a construct's ontology deserves revision, defence, or expansion.</content>
