    <id>https://doi.org/10.1111/joms.70174</id>
    <title>Place Matters: A Dynamic Multilevel Perspective on Stigma Management in French Elderly Care</title>
    <updated>2026-11-17T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Claire Le Breton, Frédéric Bally, Albane Grandazzi, Hélène Picard</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Management Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This article interrogates how ‘place’ contributes to managing stigma and its associated multilevel processes. In doing so, it addresses a literature that often treats stigma as a static attribute, overlooking the spatial and material dynamics of managing it. Drawing on a 2.5‐year case study of Help@hom, a French national initiative aimed at restructuring elderly care, we examine how spatial and material arrangements actively shape stigma management processes. We identify three interrelated mechanisms –expanding,enclosing, andfragmenting– through which Help@hom reconfigures the meanings, locations, and materialities of care. Building on these mechanisms, we theorize place as producing, sustaining, and distributing the dynamics of stigma management: place produces stigma management strategies rather than merely providing their setting, sustains stigma by relocating rather than dissolving it, and distributes it across organizational, occupational, and individual levels. Our study demonstrates that place is constitutive of stigma management, shaping how organizations enact and experience multilevel stigma processes, and that spatial and material interventions can produce unexpected cross‐level consequences. In doing so, it advances recent efforts to conceptualize stigma as relational, dynamic, and systemic, and offers a more integrated understanding of how organizations navigate complex stigma management landscapes.</content>
