    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2025.2332</id>
    <title>Does Private Equity Hurt or Improve Healthcare Value? New Evidence and Mechanisms</title>
    <updated>2026-11-17T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Minghong Yuan, Wen Wen, Indranil Bardhan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Given the significant investments by private equity (PE) firms in healthcare, it is increasingly important for stakeholders, including policymakers, care providers, and patients, to understand how PE ownership affects healthcare value, defined as the quality of care delivered relative to clinical resources used. Using longitudinal data on U.S. hospitals from 2008 to 2020, we find that PE ownership reduces healthcare value. Reductions in clinical staffing following PE acquisition partly explain this pattern: lower staffing reduces operating costs but also weakens hospitals’ capacity to maintain care quality. However, hospitals with stronger health information sharing capabilities experience substantially smaller declines in healthcare value, as these capabilities support cost reduction while mitigating care quality deterioration. Our findings offer actionable insights for healthcare stakeholders including identifying the underlying mechanisms that explain the impact of health information sharing on care value. Policymakers should evaluate PE acquisitions using a comprehensive, value-based framework which measures both care quality and costs. Hospital administrators should prioritize IT investments that strengthen information sharing across providers, whereas policymakers should promote common health data interoperability standards. Together, these efforts can help PE-owned hospitals preserve care quality under financial pressure and better align PE firms’ financial objectives with the goals of value-based care.</content>
