    <title>Campaigns That Click: Unleashing the Power of Push Notifications on Mobile Apps</title>
    <updated>2026-11-18T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Zhuping Liu, Bicheng Yang, Subodha Kumar</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Push notifications can do more than promote a single merchant: They can redirect attention across an entire mobile marketplace. Drawing on more than 200 campaigns, 60 million user-campaign observations, and two controlled experiments, this study shows that notifications substantially increase same-day engagement with advertised merchants while also generating meaningful spillovers to other merchants within and across categories. Effectiveness varies by merchant, user, message design, and delivery timing. Notifications tend to work especially well for less prominent merchants, newer users, Android users, deeper discounts, urgency cues, and carefully timed delivery. However, repeated exposure produces diminishing returns and can eventually reduce engagement as annoyance and attentional overload increase. For platform managers, the findings support managing notifications as a portfolio rather than evaluating campaigns in isolation: targeting, message design, timing, cross-merchant benefits, and cumulative frequency should be optimized jointly. Platforms should also incorporate spillover value into campaign pricing, performance reporting, and merchant communication. For policymakers and platform-governance leaders, the results underscore the importance of meaningful consent, frequency controls, and transparent notification practices that protect user attention while preserving the economic benefits of timely, relevant mobile communication.</content>
