    <title>Technology-Mediated Services in Healthcare: Evaluating Telehealth Delivery Models for Underserved Populations</title>
    <updated>2026-11-18T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Kyuyeon (Jenny) Rhee, Xiao Liu, Tan (Suparerk) Lekwijit, Pei-yu Chen</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Telehealth is often promoted as a way to expand healthcare access, particularly for underserved populations, yet its benefits are far from uniform. Our research shows that how telehealth is delivered matters. Using data from U.S. Community Health Centers, we examine two common delivery models for mental healthcare: specialist-facilitated telehealth, in which health centers connect patients with external specialists, and remote telehealth, in which patients receive care virtually from their own locations. We find that specialist-facilitated telehealth expands access, increasing the number of mental health patients served and visits, with particularly strong benefits in areas where specialists are scarce and clinical need is high. Remote telehealth, by contrast, does not increase access on average but improves adherence to standardized depression screening and follow-up protocols. Its benefits also depend on local conditions; limited device access can constrain utilization gains, whereas transportation barriers increase the value of remote care. For healthcare organizations and policymakers, these findings suggest that the benefits of telehealth depend on how and where it is implemented. Different delivery models address different barriers. Effective telehealth strategies should, therefore, match the design of care delivery to the specific constraints facing the communities that they serve.</content>
