    <title>Examining Negative Asymmetry in Employee Social Networks: A Meta-Analysis</title>
    <updated>2026-11-19T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Kameron M. Carter, Hamed Ghahremani, Eean R. Crawford, Prasad Balkundi, Robert J. Barrett</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Two decades have passed since Labianca and Brass introduced social ledger theory with its focal assumption of negative asymmetry—that the social liabilities created by negative workplace ties are more strongly related to employee outcomes than the social assets created by positive workplace ties. Yet widespread conclusions that negative workplace ties are more consequential have exceeded a surprisingly limited base of empirical tests. This article uses meta-analysis to examine this largely untested assumption by offering 22 original meta-analytic estimates drawn from 248 effect sizes across 71 samples representing 13,369 employees. At the bivariate level, both positive and negative ties have significant relationships with employee outcomes. However, evidence consistent with negative asymmetry emerged only for out-degree ties predicting task performance. In contrast, evidence more consistent with positive asymmetry emerged for in-degree ties predicting task performance and for both in-degree and out-degree ties predicting organizational attachment, although the relationships between in-degree ties and attachment are relatively small. Considerable variation exists around the effect sizes constituting these patterns, suggesting that future research is needed to consider moderating factors that may shift the relative importance of negative versus positive ties. We discuss the implications for refining social ledger theory and for helping practitioners managing social assets and liabilities in organizations.</content>
