    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/jcr/ucag042</id>
    <title>Giving More in a Non-Native Language: The Effect of Language on Monetary Donations</title>
    <updated>2026-11-19T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Marie Ozanne, Archer Yue Pan, Xinlian Zhang, Kaitlin Woolley</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Consumer Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;How does the language of a charitable appeal, whether communicated in a potential donor’s native or non-native (but proficient) language, influence monetary donations? Whereas research finds that people behave more morally in their native (vs. non-native) language, eight preregistered experiments (N = 7,447) and one archival analysis (N = 3,142) find that people donate less in response to appeals in their native language. People facing a donation decision need to determine whether and how much to give, with the appropriate action varying across situations. Situational considerations that inform this decision are more accessible in a native language, making donation decisions seem less diagnostic of the self, reducing giving. Consistent with this process, perceived self-diagnosticity mediates the effect of language on donations, and manipulating self-diagnosticity attenuates this effect. Reconciling these findings with prior research, the relationship between language and moral behavior depends on the type of moral decision under consideration: a native language promotes adherence to moral rules when decisions involve moral violations (stealing; lying), but reduces giving for donation decisions, which we refer to as moral aspirations. Beyond advancing theory on language, self-diagnosticity, and moral behavior, this research offers practical guidance to maximize charitable giving when communicating with multilingual audiences.</content>
