    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/hrm.70111</id>
    <title>Toward an Integrative Model of Layoff Effects: A Strategic Human Capital Resources Framework</title>
    <updated>2026-11-19T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Kaifeng Jiang, Qianning Liu, Dongyuan Wu, Jie Feng</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Human Resource Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Despite decades of research yielding rich theoretical insights and empirical evidence, the layoff literature remains fragmented. It lacks an overarching framework integrating findings across levels of analysis (e.g., organizational, group, and individual), stakeholder groups (e.g., layoff decision‐makers, executioners, survivors, and displaced employees), and outcomes from proximal processes to distal performance consequences. This fragmentation impedes understanding of how layoff effects unfold and why they vary across individuals and organizations. We review and synthesize layoff research and integrate insights from recent advancements in the strategic human capital resources literature. Our framework explains how layoffs first trigger an outflow of human capital and then reshape the quality, complementarity, and deployment of remaining human capital resources, while shaping the social‐psychological reactions of stakeholder groups. We further identify layoff characteristics as contingencies that determine whether layoffs produce negative or positive shifts in strategic human capital resources states, shape stakeholder reactions, and condition whether individual outcomes aggregate to the organizational level. In doing so, we delineate the organizational‐, individual‐, and cross‐level pathways through which layoffs influence individual performance and, ultimately, organizational outcomes. We offer implications for firms seeking to plan layoffs strategically, manage social and psychological processes, and leverage human capital resources before, during, and after layoffs.</content>
