    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/00018392261498222</id>
    <title>Cooperative Needs and Competitive Opportunities: Lobbying for Technology and the Expropriation of Knowledge</title>
    <updated>2026-11-19T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Michael Park</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Administrative Science Quarterly&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Despite the importance of regulations regarding new technologies, there has been limited research on the competitive dynamics that may unfold among firms engaged in lobbying. In this article, I explore the potential for knowledge expropriation when firms lobby for technology-related regulations. I propose a framework that distinguishes two processes underlying knowledge expropriation—knowledge leakage and uptake—and argue that employing lobbyists creates opportunities for both. Given the different roles of internal and external lobbyists, I predict that increases in internal lobbyists will lead to a greater risk of leakage, while increases in external lobbyists will give rise to more opportunities for uptake. I test these predictions on a unique dataset of U.S. firms that employed lobbyists to influence technology policy and the patents for which those firms applied. I use an embedding approach on the text of patents to directly detect both knowledge leakage and uptake. Overall, the results suggest that lobbying enables opportunities for knowledge expropriation because firms share information that is useful, reliable, timely, and therefore strategically valuable to competitors. In this way, my study identifies a new competitive dynamic at the intersection of firms’ political and innovation strategy.</content>
