    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261511648</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: The Role of Synchrony in the Interpersonal Effectiveness of Operations Management</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Vaneet Kaur, David A. Waldman, Salome Opoku, Shardul Phadnis</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;To achieve a more central role in the top management of firms, the evolution of operations managers involves transitioning from largely isolated technical expertise into leadership excellence that is characterized by interpersonal effectiveness. Grounded in the rich behavioral Operations Management (OM) literature, this conceptual study draws on insights from neuroscience. We argue that success in this expanded role depends on balancing OM’s traditional focus on analytical rigor and the intuitive/emotional demands of senior leadership through a framework that integratesintrapersonalandinterpersonalsynchrony.Intrapersonalsynchrony involves the neural capacity that enables a manager to achieve the cognitive and behavioral attributes that are associated withinterpersonalsynchrony. We explore how interventions like neurofeedback, along with organizational and cultural context factors, can help to develop or maximize these two forms of synchrony. Thus, an alignment of individual capacity, along with a supportive organizational and cultural context, is likely to help operations managers to move beyond a rigid analytic approach toward a broader strategic perspective based on interpersonal effectiveness, and thereby transcend technical gatekeeping and be more integrated into the strategic efforts of firms. In doing so, the study highlights the potential of neuroscience to provide insights into human aspects of managerial decision-making, which have been recognized in OM since the field’s inception over a century ago.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261511648"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</published>
