    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261511680</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Financing Selection Under Procurement Decentralization with a Quality-Uncertain Supplier</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Zhiping Lin, Qi Zhang, Di Xu, Ruixia Shi, Gangshu (George) Cai</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This article investigates how financing choice interacts with outsourcing structure and endogenous quality when a capital-constrained supplier exerts unobservable effort to improve component quality. We consider two common outsourcing structures: consignment, in which the OEM contracts directly with the supplier while outsourcing assembly to a contract manufacturer (CM), and turnkey (i.e., procurement decentralization), in which the CM intermediates component procurement. In each structure, the supplier can be financed either by a bank (bank finance) or by its downstream buyer (buyer finance). Our analysis delivers several main insights. First, we identify aSupplier’s Collateral Paradox: a higher collateral value can reduce the supplier’s equilibrium profit even as it improves product quality and increases total supply chain profit, because downstream firms exploit the supplier’s improved reliability to extract surplus. Second, we establish aFinancier’s Advantage: the downstream firm always prefers to provide buyer finance itself (the OEM under consignment and the CM under turnkey), whereas the supplier always prefers bank finance. Third, the performance implications of buyer finance depend critically on the outsourcing structure: under consignment, buyer finance strictly dominates bank finance by yielding higher equilibrium quality and greater supply chain efficiency, and the OEM fully extracts the CM’s surplus, while under turnkey, buyer finance improves quality and efficiency only when the supplier’s collateral is sufficiently high, whereas when collateral is low, bank finance can deliver higher quality and greater supply chain efficiency, reflecting the CM’s intermediary role and the resulting amplification of triple marginalization. Fourth, compared with the case without capital constraints, capital constraints alter the extent of firms’ relative procurement preferences between consignment and turnkey. We further demonstrate the robustness of these findings under five extensions—financing of effort cost, imperfect inspection, stochastic market demand, CM pricing power, and quality-dependent production cost. Overall, the results highlight that financing authority and outsourcing structure jointly shape incentives, surplus allocation, and quality outcomes in outsourced supply chains.</content>
