    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261511699</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: A Framework Addressing Supply Chain Carbon Emissions: Measurement, Reporting, and Reduction</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jing Wu, Yilin Shi, Yi Liao</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper honors Professor Christopher S. Tang’s seminal contributions to sustainable operations management by examining the pivotal yet underappreciated role of supply chains in global carbon emissions. Despite the critical importance of supply chains in contributing to overall carbon footprints, current efforts to address these emissions are often overshadowed by more visible direct emissions (Scope 1) and energy-related emissions (Scope 2). To bridge this gap, we propose an integrated measurement-reporting-reduction framework for managing supply chain carbon emissions (upstream Scope 3). Drawing on the literature, we introduce methodologies for carbon measurement (e.g., EEIO models, LCA, machine learning, and blockchain), determinants and consequences of disclosure, and strategies for reduction. In addition, we present case studies of two industrial firms, Tongwei (a Chinese photovoltaic leader) and Siemens (a German multinational technology firm), to illustrate how firms with distinct institutional contexts implement the framework in practice, highlighting the strengths, challenges, and implications. We conclude with research questions on whether disclosure mandates and carbon border measures lead to real abatement or merely shift emissions across suppliers and borders.</content>
