    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261512192</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Assessing the Quality of Illegal Copies and its Impact on Revenues and Distribution</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Anthony Koschmann, Yi Qian</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Conventional wisdom holds that illegal copies cannibalize legitimate sales, even though previous research has found mixed effects, with illegal copies acting as both a substitute and a complement. Yet relatively little is known about how variation in piracy quality affects market outcomes over a product’s lifecycle. Building on product uncertainty and production quality, we propose that higher quality copies benefit sales when product uncertainty is higher (during product launch) but hurt sales when product uncertainty is lower (during post-launch). We examine this question in the context of motion pictures by constructing an objective measure of piracy quality from technical signals embedded in pirated files, combining this data with box office performance, screen availability, and online piracy activity. An econometric framework jointly models movie screens, revenues, illegal downloads, and available illegal copies with piracy quality in both the launch and post-launch periods. We find that at launch, when less information is known about a movie, higher quality illegal copies demonstrate a positive sampling effect on revenues. In the post-launch period, however, higher quality illegal copies exhibit a negative substitution effect on revenues. The findings have operations implications, as producers can alleviate product uncertainty through some higher quality samples at product launch, while diluting the piracy market with lower quality post-launch.</content>
