    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/00222437261511889</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Making the Shelf Work for You: How Shelf Dimensions and Brand Types Influence the Shelf Placement Effect on Product Performance</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Kristopher O. Keller, Arjen van Lin</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Marketing Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This study examines how vertical and horizontal shelf position and shelf space affect product sales, and how these effects vary across brand types and shelf dimensions. Using sales data and detailed planogram changes from a leading convenience-store retailer, the analysis distinguishes primary NBs (market-leading national brands), secondary NBs (other nationally distributed brands), and PLs (private labels), and compares tall versus short and wide versus narrow shelves. On tall shelves, primary and secondary NBs perform best between the middle and top, whereas vertical position has little effect for PLs. On short shelves, NBs perform better toward the top and bottom, while PLs tend to perform better near the top. On wide shelves, primary NBs perform best slightly left of center, while horizontal position has little effect for secondary NBs and PLs. On narrow shelves, primary NBs perform best near the center, PLs left of center, and secondary NBs show only modest differences across positions. More shelf space increases sales most strongly for PLs. A shelf-optimization analysis suggests substantial model-implied sales improvements from shelf rearrangement. Overall, the findings help retailers assess and justify requests for contested premium positions and shelf space by showing which brand types benefit most across shelf types.</content>
