    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/00222429261511608</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: The Relationship Between a Salesperson’s Past and Future Performance</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Melissa Baucum, Alex R. Zablah, Nawar N. Chaker, Johannes Habel</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Marketing&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;A salesperson’s past performance is widely assumed to predict their future performance, and thus often guides managerial decision-making. Contrary to that assumption, this study uncovers why and under what conditions past performance becomes a less reliable predictor of future performance. A grounded theory study in a solution-selling context reveals that recent wins generate after-sales workload that lowers the odds of converting the next lead, particularly when those wins are complex, as occurs when customer relationships are new or procurement is formalized. Analysis of seven years of CRM data covering 3,669 sales leads at a Fortune 500 firm confirms that the link between past and future wins weakens as recent wins grow in complexity. Behavioral experiments causally confirm this attenuation effect, showing recent wins reduce effort on the next lead when those wins are complex. Finally, a single-dataset meta-analysis offers convergent evidence for workload as the underlying mechanism, revealing the relationship also weakens when workload is decoupled from winning and rooted in organizational change instead. For managers, these findings imply that using past performance to guide forecasting, quota setting, and coaching risks suboptimal decisions. They also underscore the importance of resourcing salespeople to balance after-sales responsibilities with new business development.</content>
