    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/01492063261492634</id>
    <title>Stakeholder Governance</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Romain Boulongne, Ryan Federo, Ruth V. Aguilera, Pascual Berrone, Maurizio Zollo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Stakeholder governance has become central to debates about stakeholder capitalism, yet research remains fragmented about the configurations through which non-shareholder stakeholders gain authority and firms become credibly bound to stakeholder claims. We systematically review 171 articles in management and related fields to develop a framework organized around stakeholder authority (i.e., the participatory rights or recognized standing through which stakeholders can engage in governance), and firm commitment (i.e., the obligations organizations assume toward stakeholder claims in ways that are costly to reverse or ignore). We identify five recurring configurations: (a) disclosure and evaluation; (b) monitoring and verification; (c) contracting, formal rules, and adjudication; (d) governance rights; and (e) rule-making rights. These configurations generally move from informational authority and very weak firm commitment to constitutional authority and very strong firm commitment. We also identify two off-diagonal configurations—constrained authority and embedded commitment—that reveal how authority and commitment need not align. Our framework distinguishes stakeholder governance that remains largely symbolic from arrangements that create durable constraints on organizational discretion. We conclude by outlining a research agenda on institutional settings, organizational forms, transitions across governance configurations, and the implications of learning dynamics for stakeholder authority and commitment.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/01492063261492634"/>
    <category term="Journal of Management"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</published>
