  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1230</id>
    <title>Impact of Data Privacy Regulations on Recommender Systems Performance</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Liben Chen, Meizi Zhou, Yicheng Song, Gediminas Adomavicius</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Data protection regulations like GDPR and CCPA let consumers delete personal data or opt out of collection, but companies struggle to understand how these practices affect their recommendation systems. This matters because recommendations drive substantial revenue; according to industry reports, Netflix’s system saves more than $1 billion annually, and personalized suggestions generate 35% of Amazon purchases. We analyzed how different data privacy practices impact recommendation accuracy using large-scale simulations with real-world data. A critical finding: Context matters enormously. For services where user preferences are more stable (e.g., streaming platforms), opting out barely affects recommendation quality while deleting data cuts performance dramatically. However, for platforms where preferences and needs can change frequently (e.g., e-commerce), deleting old data can actually improve recommendations while opting out severely harms them. For policymakers, this means one-size-fits-all regulations may be counterproductive. For companies, we demonstrate that data privacy practices can be redesigned: users can protect their most revealing data while maintaining close-to-optimal recommendation quality. This challenges the tradeoff between privacy and personalization, showing both could be achievable through smarter design. Companies should offer context-appropriate privacy options; regulators should recognize different platforms need different approaches.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1230"/>
    <category term="Information Systems Research"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</published>
