  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2025.1970</id>
    <title>Mobile Location-Based Recommendation Systems and Local Businesses: Evidence from the Restaurant Industry</title>
    <updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jin Soo Han, Gunwoong Lee, Keongtae Kim</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;When looking for a place to eat, how much do recommendations on your smartphone influence your choice? Can they help restaurants tucked away on side streets thrive in an age of algorithmic recommendations? We studied the launch of a location-based recommendation feature on South Korea’s largest search platform, tracking 16,720 restaurants over eight months.We find that visibility matters, but its benefits are uneven. Restaurants tucked away on side streets, far from subway stops, attracted the most additional attention when recommended by the algorithm. Yet this advantage did not translate into greater intentions to visit. Making a restaurant easier to discover did not necessarily overcome the inconvenience of reaching it.Digital information helped bridge this online-to-offline friction. Hard-to-reach restaurants with stronger online reputations and richer information, including customer reviews, photos, and menus, were better able to turn attention into visit intentions.For small businesses, visibility creates opportunities, but credible information helps turn attention into potential customers. For platforms and policymakers, location-based recommendations can bring overlooked businesses into view, but realizing their potential requires making reliable information readily available to consumers.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2025.1970"/>
    <category term="Information Systems Research"/>
    <published>2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00</published>
