    <id>https://doi.org/10.1007/s11142-026-10001-4</id>
    <title>Disclosing work-from-home flexibility to compete for talent? Evidence from job postings</title>
    <updated>2026-11-21T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Charles Ham, Rebecca N. Hann, Wenfeng Wang, Jingwen Yang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Review of Accounting Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Job postings serve as one of the primary means to communicate with prospective employees, enabling employers to highlight the benefits of the position and attract qualified applicants. As work-from-home flexibility has become increasingly prevalent, this study examines whether employers strategically disclose work-from-home flexibility in their job postings and how this disclosure is associated with hiring outcomes. Using a comprehensive sample of job postings from US employers between 2017 and 2024, we find that employers are more likely to disclose work-from-home flexibility when facing greater labor market competition and that disclosing this flexibility is associated with a higher job vacancy filling rate, especially when work-from-home information is disclosed in a more salient manner. However, we document a positive association between work-from-home disclosures and subsequent employee turnover, suggesting potential costs associated with these disclosures. These findings add to the growing literature on firms’ strategic disclosure targeted towards labor markets.</content>
