    <title>Competition for Customer Attention is Creating Oversupply Pockets in U.S. Micromobility</title>
    <updated>2026-11-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Hale Erkan, Ashish Agarwal, Kumar Muthuraman, Ioannis Stamatopoulos</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Operations Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;In This Issue: Competition for Customer Attention Is Creating Oversupply Pockets in U.S. MicromobilityU.S. cities face a substantial oversupply of electric scooters. Across nine U.S. cities, Erkan et al. find that scooters are in use just 3.9% of the time, which is half the minimum utilization target set by transportation departments. Riders choose whichever scooter is closest, so operators keep adding scooters in the same locations to avoid getting crowded out, even though existing fleets already meet nearly all demand. In Kansas City, Missouri, oversupply reaches 406%, with rider choice and competition jointly accounting for 81% of that excess. Issuing one more operator permit would increase oversupply by another 82 percentage points without increasing the fill rate. The authors recommend reducing the number of operator permits and regulating utilization. Their research has helped shape scooter regulation in Chicago, which reduced its number of operators from 10 to three, and offers other transportation departments guidance on addressing low utilization.</content>
