    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2025.00524</id>
    <title>Information Design with Supplemental Investment</title>
    <updated>2026-11-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Lin Nan, Lin Qiu, Xiaoyan Wen, Ronghuo Zheng</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Management Science&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper features a setting with two-dimensional information asymmetry on a firm’s fundamental (good or bad type) and the nature of its supplemental investment (improving upside potential, or mitigating downside risk). We examine the firm’s optimal design of the information system when the system can only address the dimension of fundamental. We show that, with only single-dimensional information asymmetry regarding the firm’s fundamental, greater informativeness on the firm’s fundamental type always improves efficiency. However, with two-dimensional information asymmetry, increasing informativeness about firm fundamental no longer guarantees higher efficiency and can be detrimental. The optimal information system trades off reducing information asymmetry and preserving firms’ incentives to invest, and it could be uninformative or optimistically biased, depending on the level of credit risk, which in our setting is higher when supplemental investment is more likely to improve upside rather than protect downside.This paper was accepted by Ranjani Krishnan, accounting.Supplemental Material: The online appendix is available at https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2025.00524 .</content>
