    <title>Deep Tangency Portfolio</title>
    <updated>2026-11-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Guanhao Feng, Liang Jiang, Junye Li, Yizhi Song, Yuanzhi Wang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Management Science&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We develop a deep learning framework to directly estimate tangency portfolio weights by augmenting benchmark factors with a long-short deep factor derived from high-dimensional characteristics. Guided by a Sharpe ratio maximization objective, the deep factor plays two key roles: (i) providing a natural hedge against benchmark risks and (ii) capturing multimarket signals to enhance the performance of the benchmark. In an empirical application to U.S. corporate bonds using 132 firm-level bond, equity, and option characteristics, the deep factor achieves an out-of-sample Sharpe ratio exceeding one, and the deep tangency portfolio benchmarked against the market factor yields a Sharpe ratio over two, outperforming portfolios spanned by common observable or latent factors. These results demonstrate that bond risk signals are fundamentally dense, requiring the nonlinear integration of multimarket information to span the efficient frontier.This paper was accepted by Agostino Capponi, finance.Funding: G. Feng acknowledges financial support from the Hong Kong Research Grants Council [Grant GRF11502023] and the National Natural Science Foundation of China [Grant 72203190]. G. Feng is partially supported by the InnoHK Initiative of the Innovation and Technology Commission of the Hong Kong SAR and the Laboratory for AI-Powered Financial Technologies. L. Jiang acknowledges financial support from the National Natural Science Foundation of China [Grant 72473027]. J. Li acknowledges financial support from the National Natural Science Foundation of China [Grant 72473026].Supplemental Material: The online appendix and data files are available at https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2023.03136 .</content>
