    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2025.1889</id>
    <title>Spoofing and Manipulating Order Books with Learning Algorithms</title>
    <updated>2026-11-24T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Alvaro Cartea, Patrick Chang, Gabriel Garcia Arenas</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Operations Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We propose a dynamic model of the limit order book to derive conditions to test whether it is optimal to manipulate the order book. Our results show that as a market maker becomes more tolerant to bearing inventory risk, optimal strategies that manipulate the book occur more frequently. Manipulation helps to revert inventory to an optimal level and to execute round-trip trades with limit orders at a higher probability than was otherwise likely to occur. Spoofing is a special case of quote-based manipulation where the market maker prefers that the manipulative limit orders are not filled. Overall, if certain conditions hold, then the optimal trading strategy is to manipulate the limit order book. We use high-frequency data to check our conditions and show that the conditions for manipulation hold in Nasdaq’s limit order book.</content>
