    <title>Optimize-via-Estimate: How to Make Finite-Sample Corrections for Sampling Uncertainty in Data-Driven Optimization</title>
    <updated>2026-11-24T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Gar Goei Loke, Taozeng Zhu, Ruiting Zuo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Operations Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;From Data to Decisions: Correcting for Uncertainty in Data-Driven OptimizationHow can decisionvmakers obtain reliable out-of-sample performance when optimization relies on limited historical data and unknown model parameters? In their operations research paper “Optimize via Estimate: How to Make Finite-Sample Corrections for Sampling Uncertainty in Data-Driven Optimization,” Loke, Zhu, and Zuo develop a novel framework to address this challenge. The authors show that conventional data-driven optimization can be fundamentally ill defined when the underlying parameter is unknown, making universally optimal decisions impossible. They instead formulate the problem as a weighted multicriteria optimization problem using a prior distribution over the uncertain parameter. The resulting optimize via estimate (OVE) procedure provides a point-wise solution that explicitly corrects for both parameter uncertainty and sampling uncertainty. The theoretical results establish conditions for prior optimality and reveal how the correction terms modify the original optimization objective. Numerical studies on newsvendor and portfolio optimization problems demonstrate that OVE can achieve near-zero ex ante regret relative to a perfect-information benchmark, highlighting its potential for more reliable data-driven decision making.</content>
