    <title>Platform Information Design and Competitive Price Targeting</title>
    <updated>2026-11-24T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Ruiqi Wu, Yufeng Huang, Nan Li</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Marketing Science&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study platform information design, in which a platform provides information about individual consumers, enabling sellers to set targeted prices. Because targetable information partitions the market and might intensify seller competition, what information should be provided remains an empirical question. We collect novel microdata from Steam, the dominant PC video game platform, and use granular consumer purchase and usage data to estimate their heterogeneous demand for video games. We then develop an equilibrium targeted pricing model to simulate counterfactual outcomes under all possible information designs. We demonstrate that the profit-maximizing information design—which distinguishes heterogeneous consumer types while accounting for the design’s equilibrium impact—yields substantially higher profits compared to demand-based heuristics that ignore the equilibrium impact.</content>
