    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2025.01507</id>
    <title>Screening, Investment Incentives, and the Welfare Trade-Offs of Privacy</title>
    <updated>2026-11-24T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Anastasios Dosis, Casey Rothschild</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Management Science&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper studies how information disclosure affects investment incentives and contracting efficiency. We analyze a class of principal-agent models in which information helps the principal screen the agent but also facilitates surplus appropriation, thereby weakening the agent’s incentive to invest before contracting. Efficient privacy regimes balance these two forces. Along the frontier of efficient information policies, more privacy is associated with stronger investment incentives and higher agent payoff. We characterize conditions under which full privacy, full disclosure, or intermediate privacy is efficient. The results provide guidance for intellectual property and innovation policy by identifying when additional privacy fosters or hinders economic efficiency and innovation.This paper was accepted by Alfonso Gambardella, business strategy.Supplemental Material: The data files are available at https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2025.01507 .</content>
