    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/jcpy.70048</id>
    <title>Consumer sickness behavior: A model for understanding consumption during illness</title>
    <updated>2026-11-24T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Felipe M. Affonso</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Consumer Psychology&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Illness is a near‐universal and economically consequential consumer state, yet consumer theory has no framework for how being sick changes consumption. Prior work has focused on external pandemic responses and not the internal biological state of being sick, and little research connects the biological mechanisms of illness (inflammation, cytokine activation) to systematic change in consumer behavior. Drawing on converging evidence from immunology, neuroscience, and consumer behavior, we develop the Consumer Sickness Behavior (CSB) model. The model proposes that illness activates an adaptive motivational program with three psychological mediators: motivational reprioritization, risk‐perception recalibration, and effort‐value recalibration. It predicts a distinctive differential‐effects pattern, the simultaneous suppression of exploratory and social consumption and amplification of comfort‐ and safety‐seeking, that neither a single feeling state nor fatigue produces. By treating illness as a compound psychological state, in which acute inflammation produces feelings and motivational changes together, CSB explains marketplace patterns that no account based on a single feeling or on fatigue predicts and offers implications for marketing, consumer welfare, and public health.</content>
