    <title>Confident and Well‐Connected: The Interplay of Line Managers'
                    &lt;scp&gt;HR&lt;/scp&gt;
                    Self‐Efficacy, Brokerage, and
                    &lt;scp&gt;HRM&lt;/scp&gt;
                    Implementation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-24T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Budy Purnawanto, Huadong Yang, Salma Raheem, Julia Brennecke</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Human Resource Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;HRM implementation, defined as line managers' execution of core HRM tasks, has been identified as a key challenge in realizing the competitive advantages associated with HRM. Integrating social cognitive and social network theories, we investigate the joint effect of line managers' HR self‐efficacy (i.e., their confidence in their ability to effectively perform HRM‐related tasks) and their positions as brokers (i.e., their role as intermediaries between otherwise disconnected contacts) in HR advice networks on HRM implementation and establish a link to team performance. Multilevel analyses were conducted using multisource survey data from 88 line managers, their 325 subordinates, and 69 of the line managers' direct superiors across six Indonesian firms. The findings reveal an inverted U‐shaped relationship between self‐efficacy and HRM implementation, with medium levels of self‐efficacy linked to more effective HRM implementation than either low or high levels. Moreover, line managers' positions as HR information brokers moderate the inverted U‐shaped relationship: The curve remains steeper at low levels of brokerage, but becomes shallower at high levels. Finally, HRM implementation mediates the joint effect of self‐efficacy and HR information brokerage on team performance. Taken together, these findings challenge the assumption of a linear relationship between HR self‐efficacy and HRM implementation, enrich understanding of the social foundations of line managers' HRM implementation, and contribute to broader discussions on the enablers and effects of HRM implementation.</content>
