  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.70140</id>
    <title>Hiring talent in technologically advanced countries: The paradox of springboarding for firms from weak intellectual property rights (
                    &lt;scp&gt;IPR&lt;/scp&gt;
                    ) regimes</title>
    <updated>2026-11-26T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Victor Cui, Qian Xiang, Jiatao Li, Jianqi Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Management Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Anecdotes suggest that multinational enterprises (MNEs) from weak intellectual property rights (IPR) regimes often leapfrog to technological leadership by recruiting talent in advanced host countries. Yet whether—and how—such a strategy works remains unclear. Using data on 486 MNEs from 29 countries operating in the United States and interviews with skilled inventors, we uncover a paradox in the springboard‐via‐hiring logic: externally, weak home‐country IPR reputation deters skilled inventors from joining, limiting recruitment; internally, even when talent is hired, home‐country‐imprinted IPR routines constrain post‐hire learning, yielding incremental improvements. These effects are mitigated by firm‐specific exposure to host‐country patent infringement litigation. Our research suggests that springboard‐via‐hiring is institution‐based, contingent on both home‐ and host‐country IPR regimes. These findings offer implications for technology policy and MNEs' strategy amid geopolitical tension.Managerial SummaryCan multinational enterprises (MNEs) from weak intellectual property rights (IPR) regimes leapfrog by hiring technical talent in advanced economies? Evidence on 486 MNEs from 29 countries operating in the US high‐technology sector suggests: weaker home‐country IPR reputations make it harder to attract skilled inventors; even after hiring, home‐imprinted IPR routines can constrain recruits' knowledge transfer, often steering outcomes toward incremental improvements. These home‐country constraints are, however, mitigated by firm‐specific exposure to US patent infringement litigation. The springboard‐via‐hiring strategy is thus institution‐based, contingent on both home‐ and host‐country IPR regimes. We accordingly offer advice on technology policy and global R&amp;D strategies for host‐country governments and MNEs amid geopolitical tension.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.70140"/>
    <category term="Strategic Management Journal"/>
    <published>2026-11-26T00:00:00+00:00</published>
