    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag130</id>
    <title>Juking the Stats: Policing, Misreporting, and Policy Evaluation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-26T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Michael Gibilisco, Carlo M Horz</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Review of Economic Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We analyze a game-theoretic model of crime and crime reporting to study the quality of crime statistics. An offender potentially engages in illicit behavior; an enforcer chooses effort and how to report outcomes. Because of signaling concerns, the enforcer may misreport. We show that multiple equilibria can exist and characterize when crime is under- or overreported. Increasing the enforcer’s costs of data manipulation can backfire, increasing misreporting in crime statistics. When calculating treatment effects of parameter changes, the effect on reported statistics will not equal the effect on true statistics, and the true effect can be under- or overestimated.</content>
