    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2025.1741</id>
    <title>Optimizing Input Data Collection for Ranking and Selection</title>
    <updated>2026-11-26T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Taeho Kim, Eunhye Song</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Operations Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Maximizing Data Efficiency in Decision-Focused Learning with SimulationSimulation optimization is crucial for decision making, but its results often depend on the parameters estimated from finite data. With a fixed budget, how much of various types of data should be collected? In “Optimizing Input Data Collection for Ranking and Selection,” Taeho Kim and Eunhye Song propose a learning framework for sequentially making these decisions. They characterize how budget allocations across input data sources and simulation experiments affect the Bayesian learning rate for selecting the optimum. This analysis guides the optimal sampling algorithm for ranking and selection (OSAR) that iteratively updates the Bayesian model for the parameters with new data, and this then informs the parameter–solution pair most important to simulate. Numerical experiments, including a food supply chain example, show that OSAR outperforms a state-of-the-art Bayesian optimization method. OSAR offers a principled guideline for spending a limited data budget on learning the problem structure that matters most for identifying the optimum.</content>
