    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/hrm.70109</id>
    <title>Growing Together or Growing Apart? The Role of Person‐Company Fit, Professional Links, and Stock‐Options Sacrifice for Employee Retention in Scale‐Ups</title>
    <updated>2026-11-26T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Petrit Ademi, Silvia Stroe</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Human Resource Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;The scale‐up phase redefines the organizational structures, activities, and role expectations that characterized the start‐up setting early venture employees originally chose to join, thereby introducing distinctive challenges and opportunities for employee retention. This study builds on job embeddedness theory and conservation of resources theory to study employment‐continuation decisions in scale‐ups. Drawing on a decision‐making experiment and interviews with scale‐up employees, we investigate the effects of person‐company fit, professional links, and stock‐options sacrifice on employees' willingness to stay at a scale‐up. Our results show that scale‐up employees assess the resource utility of these dimensions differently depending on their individual preferences. Our study thereby advances an employee‐centric understanding of retention in scale‐ups, where staying depends on whether employees continue to find value in a venture that no longer resembles the one they joined. From a practical standpoint, this research offers important guidance for developing human resource strategies in scale‐ups, which represent a key challenge after growth‐driven professionalization has occurred.</content>
