    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10422587261496319</id>
    <title>External Enablement Across Types of Environmental Change: Experimental Evidence on Onset Variance Effects</title>
    <updated>2026-11-26T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jiyoung Kim, Per Davidsson, Denis A. Grégoire</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Entrepreneurship Theory and Practice&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;A growing research stream applies the external enablement framework (EEF) to capture how macroenvironmental changes benefit entrepreneurial pursuits. To refine and nuance the EEF’s guidance of future research in this domain, we examine the provisionally suggested structural relationships among its constructs. Combining between-subjects experiments and think-aloud protocols, we show that variance in the characteristics of macroenvironmental changes—specifically, their gradual versus radical onset—influences expectations of enablement and does so similarly across types of change. Our results also uncover relationships between macroenvironmental changes and agents beyond what the EEF explicates. We discuss implications for future research on macroenvironmental impact on business.</content>
